! EX: THE EXECUTION EXCEPTION INDICATOR (EXE), and what a subject
! instruction's successful branch is NOT allowed to do.
!
! THE RULE, Reference Manual 22-6530-2 printed p.49 (PDF 52 -- PDF page
! = printed page + 3), read first-hand at 300 dpi:
!
!   "In either of the execution instructions, the subject instruction
!   may be of any type and is performed according to the normal rules
!   for an operation of its type.  However, in order that the main
!   program may have full control at all times, the subject instruction
!   is not allowed to change the state of the interruption system or
!   replace the contents of the instruction counter.  The execution
!   exception indicator (EXE) is turned on by any branch operation of a
!   subject instruction in which the condition for a successful branch
!   is met.  When indicator EXE is actuated, the branch and all
!   associated operations are not performed.  The suppressed actions
!   include the disabling in BRANCH DISABLED, the enabling in BRANCH
!   ENABLED or BRANCH ENABLED AND WAIT, the counting and refilling in
!   COUNT, BRANCH AND REFILL, and so on."
!
! and printed p.45, indicator 18, which is what settles HOW MUCH "all
! associated operations" is:
!
!   "18. Execute Exception (EXE).  An instruction was executed under
!   control of the EXECUTE or the EXECUTE INDIRECT AND COUNT operations
!   that attempted to change the contents of the instruction counter.
!   THE SUBJECT OPERATION WAS SUPPRESSED."
!
! THE DECK ORACLE is jb_ax5b routine 1262 TEST 1CC, "-TEST 1CC, CHK EX
! OF BRANCH AND EXE.", transcribed for its ENCODING as
! programs/ax5b_162_test1cc.strap.  That block cannot be run here (it
! needs SSIP, 1260's subroutines and a 64-word interrupt table), and it
! reads EXE out by INTERRUPTING; this file reads it out by branching on
! the indicator bit directly.  1CC's own header records which clauses
! of the paragraph above it leaves UNWITNESSED -- no COUNT, BRANCH AND
! REFILL and no BRANCH ENABLED AND WAIT appears as an EX subject
! anywhere in it -- and blocks D and F below are written for two of
! them.
!
! THE BLOCKS, in the order the EXE indicator forces.  EXE is indicator
! 18, inside the permanently-read-only 0-19 range, so "Z,$IND" cannot
! clear it and neither can a BRANCH ON BIT (the bit-change path is
! gated on bit >= 20).  Every "EXE must be OFF" check therefore has to
! come before anything that turns it on, and block A is that check.
!
!   A  a subject branch whose condition is NOT met.  No EXE, nothing
!      suppressed: the subject's own bit change still happens.  This is
!      1CC's "EX,I62B6" case, whose only correct outcome is to fall on
!      past the EX.
!   B  a subject branch whose condition IS met.  EXE on, no branch, and
!      the BIT CHANGE suppressed -- p.45's "the subject operation was
!      suppressed".  1CC's "EX,I62B5".
!   C  a subject UNCONDITIONAL branch.  Always successful, so always
!      EXE.  The witness is WRONGC never being reached.
!   D  a subject COUNT AND BRANCH.  The clause the manual names by name
!      and 1CC does not reach: "the counting and refilling in COUNT,
!      BRANCH AND REFILL".  X7 must come out of the EX bit-for-bit as
!      it went in -- no count, no value step.
!   E  a subject STORE INSTRUCTION COUNTER IF.  p.41: the store happens
!      "if and only if the associated branching is successful", so a
!      suppressed branch must leave the target word alone.  p.52's own
!      worked example is a SIC under EXECUTE.
!   F  a subject BRANCH ENABLED while the machine is DISABLED.  "the
!      enabling in BRANCH ENABLED ... is suppressed", so the machine
!      must still be disabled afterwards.
!   G  a subject BRANCH DISABLED while the machine is ENABLED, the
!      mirror of F, and the only block that needs the interruption
!      system running.
!
! HOW THE ENABLE STATE IS READ OUT, in F and G, without an interrupt
! handler chain: p.32's address monitoring is itself gated on the
! enable bit -- "when the interruption system is disabled, address
! monitoring is ineffective".  So a READ-ONLY BRANCH ON BIT against a
! protected word sets DF when the machine is enabled and sets nothing
! when it is disabled, and DF's mask bit is zero (the reset value) so
! it signals without suppressing anything (printed p.52 clause 3:
! "Indicator DF is actuated but does not cause suppression of the
! instruction").  programs/df_bb_readonly.strap is what establishes
! that probe; this file only uses it.
!
! WHY THERE IS AN INTERRUPT TABLE AT ALL.  EXE's mask bit is one and
! cannot be anything else (mask bits 0-19 are permanently one, p.48),
! so the moment block G enables the system a set EXE interrupts.  The
! table's entries are NOPs: StretchCore runs the one instruction at the
! effective interruption address and, on a fall-through, leaves the
! instruction counter where it was -- p.48 -- so a NOP entry turns the
! interruption off, clears the indicator that caused it, and resumes.
! That is also how EXE gets cleared before G's own EX sets it again.
!
! WHAT IS NOT WITNESSED HERE, said rather than left to be found:
!   * BRANCH ON INDICATOR as a subject at all: that is
!     programs/ex_exe_bind.strap, which is a separate file because it
!     needs an indicator it can drive both ways and because the CLEAR
!     clause it pins is an INFERENCE with no deck witness.
!   * BRANCH ENABLED AND WAIT as a subject: WAIT is not implemented at
!     all in this model, so an EX of BEW would test the enable half
!     only, which block F already covers.
!   * A Class S alert arising DURING a subject instruction.  Printed
!     p.52-53 gives that its own rule ("may not cause full suppression
!     of the instruction", and p.53 item 2b), and none of it is
!     implemented.
!   * ONE CORNER OF BLOCK E'S CLAUSE, labelled rather than guessed.  A
!     suppressed SIC drops its inner branch's indicator word, which for
!     a COUNT, BRANCH AND REFILL inner branch would have carried a DF
!     signal from its refill address.  p.52 item 1 says a suppression
!     must NOT exclude "indicators DF and IF when their mask bits are
!     zero"; p.45 item 18 says the subject operation was suppressed.
!     No deck witnesses it and no program here reaches the combination
!     (SIC + a CBR inner branch + a protected refill + under EX).  The
!     arm carries the same note.
!
! SEVEN INJECTIONS, EACH OBSERVED (not predicted).  Each was applied to
! a pristine snapshot of bsv/StretchExecute.bsv by an exact-substring
! substitution that aborts unless the anchor occurs exactly once, and
! the resulting unified diff was printed before the run -- because a
! `sed` that silently fails to match prints ALL TESTS PASSED, which
! reads exactly like an honest "this does not discriminate".  Reproduce
! any row with `python3 line_addresses.py programs/ex_exe.strap`.
!
!   BB: `takeBranch` no longer excludes    -> 5894  005603.00
!     `exeHit`, so the subject branches         WRONGB B, $
!   BB: the EXE indicator never actuated   -> 5652  005412.00
!     (`if (False)` at the setBit)              BZB, $EXE, $  (B)
!   BB: the bit change no longer gated on  -> 5654  005413.00
!     `!exeHit`                                 BZB, $X6, $   (B)
!                                               -- the subject BBZ cleared
!                                                  bit 0 after all
!   CB: the EXE guard removed from the     -> 5899  005605.40
!     CountBranch arm                           WRONGD B, $
!                                               -- and RESD never written
!   BE/BD: the enable change hoisted back  -> 5665  005420.40
!     ABOVE the EXE guard, where it used        BB, $DF, $    (F)
!     to be                                     -- DF came on, so the
!                                                  machine was ENABLED
!                                                  after a subject BE
!   SIC: the EXE guard removed from the    -> MARKE = 0x2c3a00555555555
!     Sic arm                                   (the instruction counter
!                                               WAS stored), and halted at
!                                               5902 005607.00 WRONGE B,$
!   the UNCONDITIONAL-branch arm's EXE     -> 5897  005604.40
!     guard removed                             WRONGC B, $
!
! Every one halted or failed a memory check, none survived, and the
! clean run's PASS is at half-word 5684 (005432.00).
!
! WHICH CHECKS NO ROW REACHES, said rather than left to be found.
! Block A's "BB,$EXE,$" (EXE must be OFF) is never the halt: no
! injection here makes EXE come on for a branch whose condition is not
! met, and the arm computes `exeHit` from the same pair the branch
! condition uses, so such a bug would have to be a different shape.
! Block G's "BZB,$DF,$" is likewise never the halt -- the BE/BD
! injection is caught six rows earlier, in block F.  Both are kept
! because they are the OTHER direction of their clause.
!
! Instructions used: BD, BE, LVI, LX, SX, SV, BB, BB1, BBZ, BZB, Z, EX,
! B, NOP, BEW -- all implemented.

        SLC, (8)5400.0
START   BD,  $+.32                 ! disabled; blocks A-F stay that way
!
! ---- set-up, all of it while DISABLED -----------------------------
!   The boundary registers are words 1-3, which p.31 calls permanently
!   protected, so writing them at all needs the system disabled -- the
!   reason df_bb_readonly.strap gives for the same sequence.
        LVI, $X13, TBL
        SV,  $X13, $IA             ! the interruption address, p.48
        LVI, $X13, PROT
        SV,  $X13, $LB             ! lower boundary := PROT
        LVI, $X13, ABOVE
        SV,  $X13, $UB             ! upper boundary := ABOVE
        BBZ, 3.57, $+1.0           ! boundary control := 0, signal INSIDE
        LX,  $X6, BITW             ! X6 := bit 0 one, every other bit zero
        LX,  $X7, XWC              ! X7 := value 0, count 2, refill 0
!
! ---- A. subject branch condition NOT met: no EXE ------------------
!   "BB1,$X6+.01,WRONGA" -- bit 1 of X6 is ZERO, so the branch
!   condition is not met and the instruction is not suppressed at all;
!   its bit change still happens.
        EX,  SUBJA
        BB,  $EXE, $               ! hang if EXE came on
        BB,  $X6+.01, A1           ! bit 1 must now be ONE
        B,   $                     ! hang: the subject did not execute
A1      BBZ, $X6+.01, $+1.0        ! put bit 1 back to zero
!
! ---- B. subject branch condition MET: EXE, no branch, no bit change
!   "BBZ,$X6,WRONGB" -- bit 0 of X6 is ONE, so the condition IS met.
        EX,  SUBJB
        BZB, $EXE, $               ! hang if EXE did NOT come on
        BZB, $X6,  $               ! hang if bit 0 was cleared: the bit
                                   !   change is an "associated operation"
!
! ---- C. subject UNCONDITIONAL branch ------------------------------
        EX,  SUBJC                 ! subject "B,WRONGC"
        B,   D0                    ! reached only because the branch was
                                   !   not performed
!
! ---- D. subject COUNT AND BRANCH: no counting, no refilling -------
!   X7's count is 2, so "CB+,$X7,WRONGD" would step it to 1, add 64 to
!   the value and branch.  Under EXE none of that happens, so X7 comes
!   back out identical to the XWC it was loaded from.
D0      EX,  SUBJD
        SX,  $X7, RESD             ! checked against XWC by the testbench
!
! ---- E. subject SIC: the instruction counter is not stored --------
        EX,  SUBJE                 ! subject "SIC,MARKE / B,WRONGE"
                                   !   MARKE is checked unchanged by the
                                   !   testbench
!
! ---- F. subject BRANCH ENABLED while DISABLED ---------------------
        Z,   $IND                  ! clears DF (bit 20); EXE (18) survives
        EX,  SUBJF                 ! subject "BE,WRONGF"
        BB,  PROT.0, $+1.0         ! the enable probe: read-only BB on a
                                   !   protected word
        BB,  $DF,  $               ! hang if DF came on -- that would mean
                                   !   the machine was ENABLED, i.e. the
                                   !   subject's enabling was not suppressed
!
! ---- G. subject BRANCH DISABLED while ENABLED ---------------------
        BE,  $+.32                 ! ENABLE for real.  EXE is on from B-F,
                                   !   so this interrupts at once; entry 18
                                   !   is a NOP, which clears EXE and
                                   !   resumes here (see the header)
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        Z,   $IND                  ! clear DF again
        EX,  SUBJG                 ! subject "BD,WRONGG"
        NOP                        ! slack: this model takes an
        NOP                        !   interruption at a quiescent
        NOP                        !   boundary, not p.48's instruction
        NOP                        !   boundary -- df_bb_readonly's note
        BB,  PROT.0, $+1.0         ! the enable probe again
        BZB, $DF,  $               ! hang if DF is OFF -- that would mean
                                   !   the machine was DISABLED, i.e. the
                                   !   subject's disabling was not suppressed
        BD,  $+.32                 ! disable before halting
        B,   PASS
PASS    BEW, $
!
! ---- the subjects -------------------------------------------------
!   One to a word, so that each subject's address is obvious and the
!   "WRONG" trap after it is the half-word the subject would have
!   branched to if EXE had failed to suppress the branch.
        SLC, (8)5600.0
SUBJA   BB1, $X6+.01, WRONGA       ! full word
        SLC, (8)5601.0
WRONGA  B,   $
        SLC, (8)5602.0
SUBJB   BBZ, $X6, WRONGB           ! full word
        SLC, (8)5603.0
WRONGB  B,   $
        SLC, (8)5604.0
SUBJC   B,   WRONGC
WRONGC  B,   $
        SLC, (8)5605.0
SUBJD   CB+, $X7, WRONGD
WRONGD  B,   $
        SLC, (8)5606.0
SUBJE   SIC, MARKE                 ! full word: SIC + its embedded branch
        B,   WRONGE
        SLC, (8)5607.0
WRONGE  B,   $
        SLC, (8)5610.0
SUBJF   BE,  WRONGF
WRONGF  B,   $
        SLC, (8)5611.0
SUBJG   BD,  WRONGG
WRONGG  B,   $
!
! ---- the interruption table (p.48: entry N is at TBL + N words) ---
!   Every entry a NOP, so any interruption that does fire turns its own
!   indicator off and hands control straight back.  The table is sized
!   to cover indicator 21 (IF); nothing above that can be actuated by
!   anything in this file.
        SLC, (8)5700.0
TBL     NOP
        SLC, (8)5722.0
EXERTN  NOP                        ! entry 18 = EXE (0o5700 + 0o22)
        SLC, (8)5723.0
DSRTN   NOP                        ! entry 19 = DS
        SLC, (8)5724.0
DFRTN   NOP                        ! entry 20 = DF
        SLC, (8)5725.0
IFRTN   NOP                        ! entry 21 = IF
!
! ---- data ---------------------------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)6000.0
BITW    DD(BU,64,8), 9223372036854775808   ! 2**63: IBM bit 0 only
        SLC, (8)6001.0
XWC     XW,  0, 2, 0               ! value 0, count 2, refill 0
        SLC, (8)6002.0
RESD    DD(BU,64,8), 0
        SLC, (8)6003.0
MARKE   DD(BU,64,8), (8)252525252525
!
! ---- the monitored area, [PROT, ABOVE) ----------------------------
        SLC, (8)6100.0
PROT    DD(BU,64,8), 0             ! == the lower boundary
        SLC, (8)6101.0
ABOVE   DD(BU,64,8), 0             ! == the upper boundary, first word out
        END, START
